#!/bin/sh
#
# post-commit -- reset TASK_RESUME.md to its stub.
#
# TASK_RESUME.md is the cursor for work that is IN FLIGHT and not yet committed.
# Once a commit exists, the commit message is the record of what landed, and any
# cursor text left behind now describes finished work -- a confident pointer at
# the past, which is worse than no pointer at all. That is not hypothetical: the
# file once carried a "next action" from weeks earlier, and grew to 3343 lines
# doing it. Committing therefore erases it.
#
# The stub is a FIXED text with no commit id in it, so the rewrite is a no-op
# whenever the file already holds the stub, and the worktree stays clean.
#
# That only holds if the CURSOR IS NEVER STAGED. Commit the work, not the notes
# about the work: with the stub in HEAD, the rewrite matches what is committed
# and nothing shows as modified. Stage the cursor text once (`git add -A` will)
# and HEAD now holds a cursor describing finished work -- restoring the file
# from HEAD brings it back, which is the exact failure this hook exists to
# prevent. Re-run this hook by hand and commit the stub to recover.

set -e

root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 0
cursor="$root/TASK_RESUME.md"
tmp="$cursor.post-commit-tmp"

cat > "$tmp" <<'STUB'
# TASK_RESUME

Nothing in flight.

This file is the cursor for work that is started and NOT yet committed: where a
long step was interrupted, what was already tried, what to do next. It is erased
automatically by the `post-commit` hook in `githooks/`, because once work is
committed the commit message is the record.

If you are reading this stub, there is no interrupted work. Read `git log`,
`PROJECT_STATE.md` and `TRAPS.md` instead.
STUB

# CRLF, matching .gitattributes (text=auto eol=crlf).
sed -i 's/$/\r/' "$tmp"

if cmp -s "$tmp" "$cursor"; then
  rm -f "$tmp"
  exit 0
fi

mv -f "$tmp" "$cursor"
echo "post-commit: TASK_RESUME.md reset to its stub (nothing in flight)."
